	title = {Private {Preference} for {Collective} {Goods} {Revealed} {Through} {Voting} on {Referenda}},
	volume = {65},
	issn = {0002-8282},
	url = {http://search.ebscohost.com/login.aspx?direct=true&db=bth&AN=4495999&site=ehost-live&scope=site},
	abstract = {This article examines the practical possibility of using voting data from public referenda to estimate demands for collective goods in California. Utilizing data on median levels of various population characteristics, certain empirical relationships between these characteristics and public expenditures are derived. If the assumptions on the political process are satisfied, these relationships are indicative of median voters' demands. The problem of estimating individual demands for collective goods may be approached in a second way, by examining voting behavior on referenda related specifically to the provision of public goods. This is the approach taken in the present paper. The presentation begins by developing a model of the individual's voting decision. The problem of using aggregate responses to draw inferences on individual preferences is then addressed and some operational assumptions are employed.},
