	volume = {8},
	issn = {03629805},
	doi = {10.2307/439429},
	abstract = {Accessed: 25-11-2017 16:59 UTC JSTOR is a not-for-profit service that helps scholars, researchers, and students discover, use, and build upon a wide range of content in a trusted digital archive. We use information technology and tools to increase productivity and facilitate new forms of scholarship. For more information about JSTOR, please contact support@jstor.org. Researchers usually assume that social, ethnic, and economic diversity in a constituency translate into political differences and that these political difference result in electoral competition. Previous research, using data from the 1950s and 1.960s has revealed that in Senate and House elections diverse constituencies had more compet tion than did homogeneous constituencies. This paper presents evidence that diversity in several constituency characteristics is not associated with competition in congression elections in the 1970s, even in open races in which the advantages of incumbency canno disguise the association. Electoral competition is necessary if government is to be represen-tative. According to democratic theory, elections ensure that representativ},
