	title = {Going {Beyond} the {Information} {Given}},
	doi = {10.4324/9780203088609-8},
	abstract = {A discussion of the capacity human have to go beyond the information given based on the use of generic coding systems or abstract theoretical structures that enable them to categorize objects into classes, learn the probabilistic relationships between events and make predictions. A coding system is "a set of contingently related, nonspecific categories" (222). The discussion is based on experimental data with rats and humans.The existence or lack of coding systems is an important predictor of organism's capacity to adapt and act in anenvironment. Several factors influence the acquisition of coding systems: 1. The way an individual is instructed, and their personal history; 2. At very high or very low levels of motivation generic learning is impeded; 3. Amount of training is positively related to generic learning. 4. Diversity of training (using different situations and examples) is positively related with generic learning. Suggests we should teach learning tools and not information. Criticism: 1. Odd operationalization of motivation - is hunger a high motivation condition, does the negative effect on learning stem from high motivation or from the detrimental physiological effect it might have on coherent thinking?},
