	doi = {10.2307/2110213},
	abstract = {This article is a comparative analysis of the role of party and constituency influences on the voting behavior of selected state senates meeting in regular session during the legislative year of 1959 or 1960. Specifically, it examines the incidence and suggests patterns of intra-party cohesion and inter-party conflict among the state senates and by categories of legislative issues. Voting in roll calls with opposing party majorities is compared to socio-economic and electoral data of constituencies by means of simple and multiple linear correlation analysis to determine the strength of senate-constituency links. The patterns of constituency relations to senate voting are discussed, particularly in regard to senate partisanship and the light thrown on the constituency-pressure theory of party voting.},
