	title = {Cosponsors and {Wafflers} from {A} to {Z}},
	volume = {39},
	issn = {00925853},
	doi = {10.2307/2111662},
	abstract = {A pre-voting, nonpartisan adaptation of Snyder's (1991) "vote-buying" and Groseclose's (1995) "favor-trading" theories implies that significant cosponsorship and discharge-petition behavior will be concentrated in the middle of the ideological spectrum, independent of legislators' partisan affiliations. Bill cosponsorship should be a primarily preference-based phenomenon. Waffling--defined as bill cosponsorship but refusal to sign a discharge petition for the bill--should be negatively associated with preference extremity and unaffected, at the margin, by majority party membership. Probit analysis of individual-level data. Measures of legislators' preferences electoral margin, seniority, committee membership, and party are used to predict patterns of cosponsorship and waffling on the A to Z spending plan in the 103rd House of Representatives. Consistent with the adapted Snyder/Groseclose hypotheses, cosponsorship and waffling are explained primarily by preferences, somewhat by membership on money committees, and only slightly by partisanship.},
