	issn = {03629805},
	doi = {10.2307/439874},
	abstract = {Organizational norms are maintained by social processes that range from the use of sanctions to internalized agreement. Within a particular organization, the social forces responsible for compliance reflect the authority relationship between its leaders and followers as well as the effectiveness of socialization. This paper investigates the social processes that promote party loyalty in the British House of Commons to deter- mine whether coercion or consent is more important as an influence on behavior. The paper identifies the most salient compliance mechanisms and analyzes how party, parlia- mentary rank, and exposure to socialization affect attitudes toward authority and con- formity. Party loyalty is shown to be a consensual process influenced by socialization. The analysis also indicates that party is a particularly strong influence in shaping the attitudes of British MPs},
