	issn = {00222437},
	doi = {10.2307/3151742},
	abstract = {QUESTION ORDER AND RESPONSE ORDER Order does matter. "Questions are moved from one survey to another with little regard for effects on response, and changes in the order of alternatives are likewise assumed to make no difference." p. 75: "With regard to question order, a major problem arises out of the increasing use of attitude questions as indicators of social change." "Yet there were also many instances where order effects might well have occurred, but did not. (...) , and the majority of survey results are probasbly not so delicately dependent on surrounding items." "general summary-type questions seem to be more sensitive to order than are more specific questions. (...) Thus, investigators should be particularly wary of moving general summary items around" BALANCE AND IMBALANCE IN QUESTIONS p. 199: "First, it appears to make little, if any, difference whether an item is formally balancedc or imbalanced in the sense of adding "oppose" or similar temrs to questions that already clearly imply the possiblity of a negative alternative." (!) (this has has import for analysis of past data) "Second, when substantive counterargumetns are added in support of onse side of a question, there is a shift of some respondents in the direction of the argument. The shift is not massive - i ranges from 4 to 13 precentage points in our experiment - but it is not trivial either." "Third, there is evidence that particular counterarguments appeal to particualr types of persons defined demographically. p. 201: "Some researchers believe that almost all unbalanced or even formally balanced questions are susceptible to an acquiescence bias, so that there is some general tendency toward agreement apart from content. The evidence in this chapter seemd to us not to supprot such a view, since neither formal balnacing nor shifting to a Some people... Other people... format has sustematic effects in reduding agreement." WORDING p. 296: "Paradoxically, it may be that the most obvious examples of bias in wording - the ones that would be seized on most quikly by the survey profession as grossly improper - are the least harmful in actual practice." "Evidently such effects can be especially strong where they are most subtle."},
