	abstract = {This paper considers the possibility that congressmen imputed a mandate on policies to two American presidential elections. Several tests of this thesis are applied to roll call voting in six Congresses. Indications of mandate-inspired voting are detected in sessions following the elections of 1936 and 1965, but nearly identical voting patterns turn up in ostensibly non-mandate sessions. The authors conclude that post-election shifts in support for administration-backed legislation are better explained by membership changes in the House of Representatives than by cues derived from election returns.},
