	doi = {10.2307/2111014},
	abstract = {This article reports on an examination of the movement of presidential popularity for the years 1963-1980. In particular, it compares public responsiveness to changin economic conditions, on the one hand, with responsiveness to dramatic events of a high political nature, on the other. The evidence indicates that each of thesse categories of influence plays an equal part. In addition, the analysis mesures the temporal dimension of the various substantive influences on presidential popularity. The data suggest that the identifiable influences have distinctive dynamics, a fact leading to speculation that those influences may be of different character.},
