	abstract = {Uses empirical evidence to argue that U.S. antipoverty policy would be more effective if it made greater use of labor demand policies. Reviews the wide variety of labor demand and supply policies used in the United States and other industrial countries and describes their size and history. Discusses some features of the workings of the low-wage labor market and their relevance to understanding labor supply and demand policies. Reviews the evidence on the effectiveness of various labor supply policies. Examines the effects on the low-wage labor market of various types of shocks to labor demand. Considers the effects on inflation of increases in labor demand. Reviews evidence on the effects of various specific labor demand policies, including public service employment policies, wage subsidies to for-profit employers, and economic development policies. Recommends a package of labor demand policies that would increase the employment and earnings of low income Americans. Bartik is Senior Economist at the W. E. Upjohn Institute for Employment Research. Index.},
