	doi = {10.1016/0305-750X(95)00047-G},
	abstract = {This study describes Costa Rica's complex minimum wage system and its evolution over 1976-1991, examines the trend in minimum wage to average wage in eight industries, and compares the number and characteristics of workers who earn below the minimum wage over these 16 years in the covered and uncovered sectors. We conclude that noncompliance is a major problem. On average one-third of the workers in the legally covered sector earn below the minimum wage over this period. Moreover, the same proportion earns below the minimum wage in the uncovered sectors. This evidence indicates that the law may fail its intent of assuring an adequate salary to workers without bargaining power. © 1995.},
